From: Christian Breunig <christian@breunig.cc>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 21:37:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] perf jitdump: include linux/string.h for strlcpy()

The jitdump hardening backported to 6.18.65 calls strlcpy() in jit_open()
but relies on the C library to declare it. glibc only provides strlcpy()
since 2.38, so on Debian Bookworm (glibc 2.36) building perf fails with:

  util/jitdump.c:247:17: error: implicit declaration of function 'strlcpy'
  [-Werror=implicit-function-declaration]

Include the tools copy of linux/string.h, which declares the fallback
implementation from tools/lib/string.c.

---
--- a/tools/perf/util/jitdump.c	2026-11-23 21:37:40.115313636 +0100
+++ b/tools/perf/util/jitdump.c	2026-11-23 21:37:40.117313651 +0100
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <linux/stringify.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
 
 #include "event.h"
 #include "debug.h"
